aystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}\\,dx\\,dy.\n$$"] [17592186134201 :question/problem "If $u=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,,$ $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,,$ and $u\\cdot u=15,$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}u)}{u\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}v)}=\n$$"] [17592186134208 :question/problem "Let $$\n\\displaystyle u=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$ be vectors such that $v$ and $w$ are orthogonal. Find $(u+9v)\\cdot w+v\\cdot (u+7w).$"] [17592186134212 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\,i+(x+1)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=3t\\,i+(2+t)\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1].$"] [17592186134213 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve $y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)$ for $x\\in [0,1].$"] [17592186134215 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} &=1 \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}=-\\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{3}{10}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{9}{10}+\\frac{1}{10}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{9}{10}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=4+9+1=14\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{12}{25}-\\frac{12}{25}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{9}{25}+\\frac{16}{25}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{16}{25}+\\frac{9}{25}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"."] [17592186134221 :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x = [x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}]^{T}$, extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of $R^{3}$. What is the value of $\\begin{vmatrix}x_{1} & + & x_{2} & + & x_{3}\\end{vmatrix}$?\n$$\n\\displaystyle U = {\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$"] [17592186134222 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\n1. $$\n\\displaystyle U = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle V = {\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n3. $W = {\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}$"] [17592186134236 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{3}-2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [17592186134248 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vector that spans the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}.$ \n\nA vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one), so ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the equation, we get $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$ \nSo, every vector in ${H}^{⊥}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$ is a basis of ${H}^{⊥}.$"] [17592186134249 :question/problem "Given the vector $u = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$, which of the following vectors are orthogonal to $u$?\n$$\n\\displaystyle v = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}, w = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186134251 :question/problem "Given the vector $u = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$, which of the following vectors are orthogonal to $u$?\n$$\n\\displaystyle v = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, w = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186134265 :question/problem "![](images/9b/9beeb093e1e89547d506a23471d33007fe50a43d277f4454c69cccf5d907f508.png)\n\nGiven that $R$ is the type II region shown above,\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∭_(R)2zdV =\n$$"] [17592186134266 :question/problem "Consider the type II region $R$, given by\n$R = {(x, y, z): (y, z) ∈ D, 0 \\le x \\le y - z}$,\nwhere $D$ is some finite region in the $yz$-plane. Then,\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∭_(R)f(x, y, z)dV =\n$$"] [17592186134267 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∭_(R)6xdV\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type II region given by\n\n$R = {(x, y, z): - 1 \\le y \\le 1, 0 \\le z \\le 3, y^{2} \\le x \\le 2 - y^{2}}$."] [17592186134271 :question/problem "![](images/bc/bc1b0ea463b0f753a2c65784fa5d46203cde8f457116c481301bf3f20ce82f54.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}4y\\,dV\n$$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"] [17592186134274 :question/problem "The triple integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∭_(R)f(x, y, z)dV = \\displaystyle \\int_{∗}^{∗}\\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{b}\\displaystyle \\int_{∗}^{∗}f(x, y, z)dxdzdy\n$$\nis defined over the type II region $R$, given by\n$R = {(x, y, z): 0 \\le y \\le 1, 1 \\le z \\le 2y, 0 \\le x \\le \\sqrt{9 - y^{2} - z^{2}}}$.\nWhat is $a + b$?"] [17592186134276 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{2}(x-y)$ and the curve $C$ is parametrized \n by $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+t,\\,{t}^{2}-t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\sqrt{2}]?$"] [17592186134277 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x, y) = 3x$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $$\n\\displaystyle r(t) = ⟨\\frac{4t^{3/2}}{3}, \\frac{t^{2}}{2} - t⟩\n$$ for $t ∈ [0, 1]$."] [17592186134280 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above, which is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2(2{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-5{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{-5}{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186134283 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\n- $U={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}$\n- $V={\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}$\n- $$\n\\displaystyle W={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$"] [17592186134283 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$\n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}}$, respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=0+0=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=1+0=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=0+1=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0+0=0\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=0-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ only\"."] [17592186134286 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0,\\,{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\\,{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0.\n$$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=5-5=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- The set $V$ cannot be orthogonal since it contains  $3$ vectors from ${R}^{2}$ (in which we can have an orthogonal set with at most $2$ non-zero vectors). So, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=4+0-4=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"."] [17592186134290 :question/problem "![](images/14/14725d6b99f88b828b4b360fd344a65a7a443a8eda859c93c1c0d405dbc4c035.png)\n\nGiven that $R$ is the type III region shown above,\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∭_(R)2zdV =\n$$"] [17592186134292 :question/problem "Consider the type III region $R$, given by\n$S = {(x, y, z): (x, z) ∈ D, 2x \\le y \\le x + z}$,\nwhere $D$ is some finite region in the $xz$-plane. Then,\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∭_(S)f(x, y, z)dV =\n$$"] [17592186134297 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the lines $y=2x$ and $y=-x$ from $y=0$ to $y=4.$ What is $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dA\n$$ expressed as a repeated integral?"] [17592186134298 :question/problem "![](images/c7/c752eb32fc94ae630ef3998c81a6b9b0af5c222a367f87db8bbacb877716c16c.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $x=2-y,\\,$ $x=\\frac{y}{2},$ and $y=0,$ as shown above, then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}xy\\,dA=\n$$"] [17592186134299 :question/problem "![](images/43/43abb2cc063dcceb31d6710a11996b9b62fd2d889adca133d9105198f9c7cd07.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $x = y^{2}$, $x = 6 - y$, and $y = 0$, as shown above, then $$\n\\displaystyle ∬_(D)f(x, y)dA =\n$$"] [17592186134301 :question/problem "Verify whether the vectors $a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}$ are linearly independent, and find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the vectors ${a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}}$, where\n$$\n\\displaystyle a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, a_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186134306 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y=2x$ and $y={x}^{2}$ above the line $y=2.$ What is $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA\\,\n$$ expressed as a repeated integral?"] [17592186134307 :question/problem "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=2x$ and $y={x}^{2},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=\n$$"] [17592186134308 :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e05b575c22c78000a1ec235bb82cbfad3cd0cdfc6db370f19f094799b305949.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=2{x}^{2}$ and $y=1+{x}^{2},$ as shown above, then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=\n$$"] [17592186134309 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-3)+0\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=2,$ $c=-1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2.\n$$"] [17592186134310 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is an orthonormal basis of $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ $b=0,$ $c=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}|+|\\,0\\,|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}|=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"] [17592186134311 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot (-2)+(-3)\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B={{u}_{1},{u}_{2}}={\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$ is an orthonormal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{2}{3},$ $b=-\\frac{2}{3},$ $c=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\frac{2}{3}|+|-\\frac{2}{3}|+|-\\frac{1}{3}|=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186134312 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 0}{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{1}{2},$ $b=\\frac{3}{2},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=3.\n$$"] [17592186134315 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, Q = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{42}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{42}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A = QR$ is a QR factorization of $A$."] [17592186134316 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\overset{A}{\\overbrace{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & - 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}} = \\overset{Q}{\\overbrace{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{ {q}_{22}} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{array} \\right]}} \\overset{R}{\\overbrace{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{ {r}_{22}}\\end{array} \\right]}} .\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns $a_{1}, a_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns $q_{1}, q_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A$, what is the value of $q_{22} \\cdot r_{22}$?"] [17592186134320 :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d66848f6030504013f8670ab0c8885b5276c833f2e420151e146fcff823ba6f.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $y=3-x,$ $y=0,$ and $x=0,$ as shown above, then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}y{e}^{x}\\,dA=\n$$"] [17592186134324 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\left(-\\frac{12}{6}\\right)\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & -2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & -2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle {q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{12}=\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)\\cdot (-2\\sqrt{6})=2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"] [17592186134326 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\left(-\\frac{9}{9}\\right)\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle {q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}=\\left(-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\cdot (-3)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$"] [17592186134330 :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186134334 :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A = QR$, find the least-squares solution to $Ax = b$, where\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & -5 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{5} & -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 1\\end{bmatrix}, R = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}\\begin{bmatrix}6\\sqrt{5} & -4\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186134336 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-2)\\cdot 1+(-5)\\cdot 5+0\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=-1,$ $c=1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{-1}{1}=-1.\n$$"] [17592186134337 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{5\\cdot 4+5\\cdot 4+(-2)\\cdot 2}{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $c=-4,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{a}=\\frac{-4}{1}=-4.\n$$"] [17592186134338 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)(x^{2} + y^{2})ds,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $r(t) = 3(\\cos t + t\\sin t)i + 3(\\sin t - t\\cos t)j$ for $0 \\le t \\le 2π$."] [17592186134358 :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-5$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the line $y = β_{0} + β_{1}x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{1}\\end{bmatrix} = (X^{T}X)^{-1}X^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?"] [17592186134359 :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-5$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y = β_{0} + β_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n$$\n\\displaystyle (X^{T}X)^{-1} = \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, X^{T}y = \\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186134365 :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax = b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are shown below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax - b‖$.\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186134367 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,{x}^{2}-2y⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the square $0\\le x\\le \\pi ,0\\le y\\le \\pi ,$ traversed counterclockwise."] [17592186134375 :question/problem "Consider the square $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,3)$ and $(-1,3),$ traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$  where $F(x,y)=⟨3xy,{x}^{2}y⟩,$ as a repeated integral."] [17592186134379 :question/problem "Let $V = R_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product\n$⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ = x(1)y(1) + x(2)y(2) + x(3)y(3)$.\nFind $⟨x(t), y(t)⟩$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle x(t) = 1 - t + t^{2}, y(t) = t - t^{2}.\n$$"] [17592186134380 :question/problem "An inner product on $V = R^{3}$ is given by $⟨x, y⟩ = 2x_{1}y_{1} + 2x_{2}y_{2} + 6x_{3}y_{3}$. Find $⟨x, y⟩$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, y = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186134388 :question/problem "| $x_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$25$** | **$32$** | **$42$** | **$15$** |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $7$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y = β_{0} + β_{1}x_{1} + β_{2}x_{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multi linear regression as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{1} \\\\ β_{2}\\end{bmatrix} = (X^{T}X)^{-1}X^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?"] [17592186134416 :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are surds?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{5}}, \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{64}}, \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{8}{27}}\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186134426 :question/worked-solution "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{2}$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nFrom the diagram we see that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {i}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ \nand \n$$\n\\displaystyle {j}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186134431 :question/worked-solution "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{2}$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nFrom the diagram we see that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {i}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ \nand \n$$\n\\displaystyle {j}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186134498 :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} &=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=5{x}_{3},$ and from the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ 5{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=4$, we get the vector \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=3,$ $b=20,$ and $c=4.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b+c}{a}=\\frac{20+4}{3}=8.$"] [17592186134499 :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} &=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the vector \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=1,$ $b=1,$ and $c=2.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{c}{a+b}=\\frac{2}{1+1}=1.$"] [17592186134504 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xy$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(x,y)=1$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4-x-y$” and statement “${f}_{1}(x,y)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=2$” are false. We have\n\n- the *bottom* surface $z={f}_{1}(x,y)=0$, and\n\n- the *top* surface $z={f}_{2}(x,y)=4-x-y.$\n\n![](images/cd/cd218d4c4ef62d63ae2954c0974fb25a95257d9fc322834b0dec4aae3e8e99fc.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ only."] [17592186134512 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)4yz\\cos xds,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t) = ti + \\sin tj + k$ for $0 < t < \\frac{π}{2}$."] [17592186134513 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)(x^{3} - y)ds,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t) = 2ti + (2 - t)j$ for $-1 \\le t \\le 2$?"] [17592186134517 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $x=1,$ $x=2,$ and the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=2{x}^{2}.$ What is $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dA\n$$ expressed as a repeated integral?"] [17592186134518 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vector of $U$ as ${u}_{1}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$, we need to find a second vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0\\,⟹\\,\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we obtain the vector \n$$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}=\\sqrt{13}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle |{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|=|\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}|+|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}|=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{13}}.\n$$"] [17592186134519 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,dA,\n$$ where $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le 2y}.$"] [17592186134520 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{-x^{2} - 2}^{2 - 2x^{2}}6x^{3}dydx.\n$$"] [17592186134545 :question/problem "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace spanned by $$\n\\displaystyle a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186134545 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nIn our case, $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(6)^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134557 :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )).\n$$*"] [17592186134561 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,t⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =0+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “$S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product” is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""] [17592186134566 :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right),t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for $F=x{y}^{2}\\,i+{x}^{2}y\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*"] [17592186134567 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,t,2t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [17592186135043 :tutorial/content "If a vector $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to all the vectors in a set $W,$ we say that $\\mathbf{u}$ is **orthogonal to $W,$** and we write $\\mathbf{u} \\perp W.$\n\nFor example, consider the set of vectors $$\n\\displaystyle W=\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}.\n$$ Can we find a *non-zero* vector $\\mathbf{u}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{array} \\right]}$ that is orthogonal to both vectors in this set?\n\nWe know that if such a vector $\\mathbf u$ does exist, then the dot product of $\\mathbf u$ with each vector in the set will be zero:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} &=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo let's calculate these dot products:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the components of $\\mathbf{u},$ we just need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNotice that the system's matrix is already in echelon form and has $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). So, $x_3$ is a free variable. \nNow, from the second equation, we have $x_2=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain $x_1=0.$ \n\nSetting $x_3=1,$ we get the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have found a vector $\\mathbf{u}$ that is orthogonal to both $\\mathbf{w}_1$ and $\\mathbf{w}_2.$\n\n![](images/a6/a64d75a0f21a4238c4b297f890d4a3202917d831c9d80427cffcc75dcdf343a0.png)"] [17592186135054 :tutorial/content "The **Gram-Schmidt process** is an algorithm that we can use to construct an *orthogonal* basis if we are given a non-orthogonal basis to start with.\n\nFor example, consider the basis $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ for the subspace $V=\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]},\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{a}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ 6\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the above basis to construct an *orthogonal* basis for the given $2$-dimensional subspace. To do this, we use the Gram-Schmidt process, which involves $2$ steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Set ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}=\\mathbf{a}_1.$\n\n- **Step 2:** Find a vector $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2$ in $V$ that's orthogonal to $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1.$ This can be achieved by computing\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}\\,-\\,{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/67/678cc056e3c9d3fbf17423ac591b3e85d84ee8060c0637840fe1c4c9e87aa878.png)\n\nNotice that $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2$ is written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1(={\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1})$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$ So this means that ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\in V,$ and also ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\perp {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}.$\nMoreover, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\} = V = \\textrm{Span}\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\}.\n$$\n\nAfter applying the Gram-Schmidt process, we get the set $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}\\},$ which is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nSo, in our example, we start by setting $\\mathbf{v}_1=\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$ Then, we find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ that's orthogonal to $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 1+6\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2\\} = \\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}5 \\\\ 5\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$"] [17592186135080 :tutorial/content "A **line integral** $$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s\n$$ is similar to a definite integral, but for a function of multiple variables along a general path $C.$\n\nTo understand the intuition behind line integrals, remember that the definite integral $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$ can be interpreted as the (signed) area bounded between the function $y=f(x)$ and the $x$-axis between $x=a$ and $x=b,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/93/93ce31caadb9d753a13acc2f25b898738aa67b70f1374816fccfc0cf3be21b7a.png)\n\nIntuitively, we can think of the integral as the result of summing the areas of many small rectangles that we accumulate as we move along the $x$-axis on the **path** from $x=a$ to $x=b.$\n\nWe can also draw paths as general curves in the plane. For example, consider the path $C,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 2\\cos t, 2\\sin t \\rangle, \\qquad t\\in \\left[ 0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right].\n$$\n\nThis path represents a quarter circle of radius $2$ traversed counterclockwise from $(2,0)$ to $(0,2)$ in the first quadrant:\n\n![](images/6d/6d933c280ebb530aefaadc1b5ef927e39603635960348255045c138ee9bab366.png)\n\nLet $f(x,y)= 3 - \\sin (2xy) .$ If we sum the areas of the thin rectangles that are formed by the curve $C$ and the surface $z=f(x,y)$ as we move along the curve $C$ from $(2,0)$ to $(0,2),$ we get a *signed area,* as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/4c/4c3b973883d1943eebc47d168f6c2fcf00cf50702ce9afdfec7d5031735bbbff.png)\n\nThis signed area is equal to the line integral $$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nFinally, we sometimes call $$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s\n$$ the line integral of $f$ **with respect to arc length.** Other types of line integral are possible, as we'll see soon."] [17592186138076 :question/worked-solution "The red segment contains all values between  $-2$ and $\\dfrac{3}{2}.$ In addition,\n\n- the closed circle at $-2$ implies that $x$ can be equal to $-2,$ while\n\n- the closed circle at $\\dfrac32$ implies that $x$ can be equal to $\\dfrac32.$\n\nTherefore, the highlighted region represents  $$\n\\displaystyle -2 \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue} \\leq }  x \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue} \\leq }  \\dfrac32 .\n$$"] [17592186138085 :question/worked-solution "To add two mixed numbers, we add\n\n- whole numbers to whole numbers, and\n- fractions to fractions.\n\nFirst, we notice that the first fraction\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac13\\right)\n$$\n\nhas a whole number part of zero.\n\nWith that in mind, let's add our numbers.\n\n- Adding the whole numbers, we get\n$0 + 5 = {\\color{red}5}$.\n- Adding the fractions, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac 1 3 + \\dfrac 1 3 = \\dfrac{1+1}{3} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 2 3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine our results:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 3 + 5\\,\\dfrac 1 3 = {\\color{red}5} + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 2 3}} =5\\,\\dfrac 2 3\n$$"] [17592186138086 :question/worked-solution "To add two mixed numbers, we add\n\n- whole numbers to whole numbers, and\n- fractions to fractions.\n\nWith that in mind let's add our mixed numbers.\n\n- Adding the whole numbers, we get\n$2 + 4 = {\\color{red}{6}}$.\n- Adding the fractions, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac 5 {10} + \\dfrac 3 {10} = \\dfrac{5+3}{10} = \\dfrac 8 {10}.\n$$\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{8}{10} = \\dfrac{8\\div 2}{10\\div 2} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{4}{5}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 5 {10} + 4\\,\\dfrac 3 {10} = {\\color{red}{6}} + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 4 5}} = 6\\,\\dfrac 4 5\n$$"] [17592186138087 :question/worked-solution "To add two mixed numbers, we add\n\n- whole numbers to whole numbers, and\n- fractions to fractions.\n\nWith that in mind let's add our mixed numbers.\n\n- Adding the whole numbers, we get\n$2+2 = {\\color{red}{4}}$.\n- Adding the fractions, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac 1 4 + \\dfrac 3 4 = \\dfrac{1+3}{4} = \\dfrac 4 4 = {\\color{blue}{1}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 4 + 2\\,\\dfrac 3 4 = {\\color{red}{4}} + {\\color{blue}{1}} = 5\n$$"] [17592186138091 :question/worked-solution "Whenever we write a decimal as a mixed number, our mixed number will have\n\n- a *whole number part* that corresponds to the whole number to the left of the decimal point, and\n- a *fractional part* that corresponds to the decimal part to the right of the decimal point.\n\nWith that in mind, let's write ${\\color{blue}51}.\\!{\\color{red}4}$ as a mixed number.\n\n- The whole number part is ${\\color{blue}51}$.\n- The decimal part has $1$ decimal place, so we can write it as a fraction in the following way:\n$0.4= \\dfrac{4}{10}$\nNow we reduce the fraction to its simplest form by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{4}{10} = \\dfrac{4 \\div 2}{10 \\div 2} = {\\color{red}\\dfrac{2}{5}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the given decimal as the following mixed number:\n\n$$\n51.4 = 51 \\, \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$"] [17592186138092 :question/worked-solution "To estimate the total amount that Michael paid, we need to estimate the product $13 \\times 17$.\n\nWe round both factors to the nearest ten:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 13\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 17 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 10 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}20\n$$\nSo, we now need to find the value of $10 \\times 20$.\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero parts and the blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}10 & ⟶10 \\\\ 20 & ⟶20\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $2$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{1} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{2} = \\color{red}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n${\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}00}$\n\nTherefore, Michael paid approximately $\\[math]200$ for the T-shirts."] [17592186138093 :question/worked-solution "To estimate the total number of tools produced, we need to estimate the product $694 \\times 38$.\n\nWe round both factors to the nearest ten, as required:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 694\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 38 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 690 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}40\n$$\nHence, we need to find the value of $690 \\times 40$.\n\nFirst, we figure out non-zero parts and blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}690 & ⟶690 \\\\ 40 & ⟶40\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $2$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts. We can do this using an area model:\n\n![](images/fa/faa4a2b25fbbee9445b34bbbc9054e140bd48a6ffc296f71d37afdbaf4a35837.png)\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n69\\times 4 = 240+36 = {\\color{red}{276}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}276}{\\color{blue}00} = 27,600\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tools factory produced approximately $27,600$ tools."] [17592186138094 :question/worked-solution "We round the first factor to the nearest ten and the second factor to the nearest hundred:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 23\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 785 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 20 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}800\n$$\nHence, we need to find the value of $20 \\times 800$.\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero parts and the blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}20 & ⟶20 \\\\ 800 & ⟶800\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $3$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{2} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{8} = \\color{red}{16}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}16}{\\color{blue}000} = 16,000\n$$\n\nTherefore, $23 \\times 785$ is approximately $16,000$."] [17592186138095 :question/worked-solution "We round the first factor to the nearest thousand and the second factor to the nearest ten:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 3, 586\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 43 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 4, 000 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}40\n$$\nHence, we need to find the value of $4,000 \\times 40$.\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero parts and the blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}4, 000 & ⟶4000 \\\\ 40 & ⟶40\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $4$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{4} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{4} = \\color{red}{16}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}16}{\\color{blue}0000} = 160,000\n$$\n\nSo, the estimate is $160,000$."] [17592186138099 :question/worked-solution "Whenever we write a mixed number as a decimal, our decimal will have\n\n- a *whole number part* to the left of the decimal point, corresponding to the whole number, and\n- a *decimal part* to the right of the decimal point, corresponding to the fraction.\n\nWith that in mind, let's write\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}53} \\,{\\color{red}\\dfrac{3}{50}}\n$$\n\nas a decimal.\n\n- The whole number part is ${\\color{blue}53}$.\n- For the fractional part $\\dfrac{3}{50}$, we can make a denominator of $100$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{3}{50} = \\dfrac{3\\times 2}{50 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{6}{100}\n$$\nSo, the fractional part can be represented as a decimal with $2$ decimal places:\n$\\dfrac{\\color{red}6}{100} =0.{\\color{red}06}$\n\nTherefore, we can express our mixed number as a decimal as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}53} \\,{\\color{red}\\dfrac{3}{50}} = {\\color{blue}{53.}}{\\color{red}{06}}\n$$"] [17592186138105 :question/worked-solution "A four-digit whole number is in expanded form when it is written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{thousands} +\\textrm{hundreds} + \\textrm{tens} + \\textrm{ones}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our numbers in turn.\n\n- The number $3,010 + 100 + 8$ is *not* written in expanded form because the first term ($3,010$) is a mix of \"thousands\" and \"tens.\"\n- The number $3,000 + 110 + 8$ is *not* written in expanded form because the second term ($110$) is a mix of \"hundreds\" and \"tens.\"\n\n- The number $3,000 + 100 + 10 + 8$ is written in expanded form because\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\color{purple}{3}}000+{\\color{blue}{1}}00+{\\color{red}{1}}0+{\\color{magenta}{8}} = {\\color{purple}{3}}\\:\\textrm{thousands} + {\\color{blue}{1}}\\:\\textrm{hundred} + {\\color{red}{1}}\\:\\textrm{ten} + {\\color{magenta}{8}}\\:\\textrm{ones}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [17592186138123 :question/worked-solution "To add two mixed numbers, we add whole numbers to whole numbers and fractions to fractions.\n\n**Step 1**: Adding the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n2+5 = {\\color{red}{7}}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Now we add the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6} + \\dfrac 1 8\n$$\n\nTo add the fractions, we need to find the least common multiple of the denominators ($6$ and $8$).\n\n- Multiples of $6{:}$ $\\quad 6,12,18,{\\color{purple}{24}},\\ldots$\n- Multiples of $8{:}$ $\\quad 8,16,{\\color{purple}{24}},\\ldots$\n\nTherefore, the least common denominator of $6$ and $8$ is ${\\color{purple}{24}}$.\n\n- To put $\\dfrac 1 6$ over a denominator of $24$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $4$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{6} = \\dfrac{1\\times 4}{6 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{4}{24}\n$$\n- To put $\\dfrac 1 8$ over a denominator of $24$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{8} = \\dfrac{1\\times 3}{8 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{3}{24}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we add the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{8} = \\frac{4}{24} + \\frac{3}{24} = \\frac{7}{24}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We combine the results to get our final answer:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{7}} + {\\color{blue} \\dfrac{7}{24}} = 7\\,\\dfrac {7}{24}\n$$"] [17592186138132 :question/problem "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{4}{\\color{blue}15} + \\dfrac 1{\\color{red}5}\n$$?"] [17592186138133 :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{\\color{red}3} + \\dfrac 1{\\color{blue}5}.\n$$"] [17592186138140 :question/problem "What is the missing number in the following equality?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 2\\,\\dfrac{2}{3} - 2\\,\\dfrac 1 4 = \\dfrac{\\,\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}}}{12}\n$$"] [17592186138141 :question/worked-solution "To subtract two mixed numbers, we subtract whole numbers from whole numbers and fractions from fractions.\n\n**Step 1**: Subtracting the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n5-3 = {\\color{red}{2}}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Now we subtract the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} - \\dfrac 1 6\n$$\n\nTo subtract the fractions, we need to find the least common multiple of the denominators ($4$ and $6$).\n\n- Multiples of 4: $\\quad 4,8,{\\color{purple}{12}},\\ldots$\n- Multiples of 6: $\\quad 6,{\\color{purple}{12}},\\ldots$\n\nTherefore, the least common denominator of $4$ and $6$ is ${\\color{purple}{12}}$.\n\n- To put $\\dfrac 3 4$ over a denominator of $12$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{3\\times 3}{4 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{9}{12}\n$$\n- To put $\\dfrac 1 6$ over a denominator of $12$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{6} = \\dfrac{1\\times 2}{6 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{2}{12}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4} - \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{9}{12} - \\frac{2}{12} = \\frac{7}{12}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We now combine the results:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2} + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{12}}} = {\\color{red}{2}}\\,{\\color{blue} \\dfrac{7}{12}}\n$$"] [17592186138142 :question/worked-solution "We round both factors to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 7.7\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 5.2 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 8 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}5\n$$\n\nSo, we need to find the value of $8 \\times 5$. Computing the product, we get:\n\n$$\n8 \\times 5 = 40\n$$\n\nTherefore, $7.7 \\times 5.2$ is approximately $40$."] [17592186138143 :question/worked-solution "We round both factors to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 6.15\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 8.64 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 6 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}9\n$$\n\nSo, we need to find the value of $6 \\times 9$. Computing the product, we get:\n\n$$\n6 \\times 9 = 54\n$$\n\nTherefore, $6.15 \\times 8.64$ is approximately $54$."] [17592186138190 :question/worked-solution "There are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $5.98$ and there are $\\color{blue}3$ decimal places in $0.432$.\n\nTo subtract the numbers, they both need the same number of decimals. Therefore, we rewrite the first number so that it has $\\color{blue}3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n5.98=5.98{\\color{red}0}\n$$\n\nNow, we line up the decimal points:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 5\\begin{vmatrix}. & 9 & 8 & 0 \\\\ - & 0 &. & 4\\end{vmatrix}3 \\mid 2;\n$$.\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{ccccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\